  {
    "episode_id": 92596,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92596.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "That's exactly right. That's exactly right. And I can speak for Virginia. There's slight variations on how things go. But, you know, at the point in time when the polls closed at 7 p.m. on election day, Virginia's 45 days of early voting. So we had 45 days of early voting plus election day. We knew down to the, you know, exact number how many ballots had been cast. And so, you know, be it in the early votes, the mail-in votes were certainly a day of election. We know how many votes had been cast. And you essentially know how many more votes you need to wait for. And so I think to the topic you're bringing up in terms of the concern and the way that it's even reported election results and as though it's an ongoing thing, it's just a waiting game. If you know that X number of votes have been cast, there's certain points where you know you could have crossed a threshold that your opponent can't overtake you even with the votes that are left, but you always know how many more there are to count or to tally.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
