  {
    "episode_id": 92596,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92596.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "And that was an exercise that was intended to keep you safe, make sure you were making the most of what were infrequent opportunities to collect information on a particular topic, and importantly keep the person you're meeting with very, very safe. And so I come from a professional background where people are supposed to, you know, every single day question you, shoot holes in your argument, play devil's advocate, whatever you want to call it, And so I think there's tremendous value in that. And within the political frame, you know, in a two party system, that's the devil's advocate is between the two parties. And I've had the experience multiple times of going to some of my Republican counterparts, you know, and sometimes quite seriously, sometimes with a little tongue in cheek saying, Okay, this is my fabulous idea for piece of legislation from your conservative or your Republican or your frame representing your state very different from mine. What do you like about? What do you dislike about it?",
