  {
    "episode_id": 92596,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92596.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Well, there's a, there's a level of, um, uh, and I, I would call it an, a kind of an unfortunate perception that, you know, if I think ABCD is the absolute best thing. And someone says, you know, I can agree with you on A, B and C. Yeah. On D, there's a, well, you're giving in, you're selling short, you're giving up, you're not insert sort of the ideologically sort of pure mentality. But the bottom line is if A, B, C, D makes people's lives better, helps people, helps our country, keeps our country safe, strengthens the economy, creates opportunity for people, keeps kids fed, whatever does right by people and for the country. Then why wouldn't I do A, B, and C? And then say, look how great that was. Now, do y'all want to try D now? Great. And I have been struck at times because to the CIA, what we were having meetings, we have what's called the magic minute where you begin every single meeting with an asset, where you talk about what happens if somebody interrupts our meeting, what's our game plan, what's our excuse for meeting here, what's the reason we're together? If we have to split up right now, when are we meeting again? And if that doesn't work, when are we meeting again? And everything that you do is predicated on the fact that you might need to move to plan B your plan C,",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
