  {
    "episode_id": 92596,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92596.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 99,
    "turn_text": "they say, oh my gosh, we love the postal inspectors because, uh, you know, again, it's a cadre of people that kind of work for an agency with a semi-confusing name and, um, and nobody really knows what it is that they do, but basically it's a federal law enforcement agency, the oldest in the country that has jurisdiction over any and a whole array of crimes that relate to the US mail service. And so I worked money laundering cases, particularly tremendous amounts of money laundering, at least at that time occurring with postal money orders. So there's your nexus. I work narcotics cases. So anything related to drug trafficking that goes through the mail, whether it's the actual drugs themselves or You get a bill for the storage unit you rented where you're keeping your drugs like anything can become a nexus. I worked a lot of joint cases with the FBI. And frankly, for a younger professional, and this is the example of where everything really works out well in the end, the experience that I had. So essentially when I had been applying, I had CIA applied around to a whole host of federal agencies. And the inspection service moved pretty quickly. And I had been very interested in the law enforcement side. I come from a law enforcement family. My father actually was a postal inspector, which is why I have such a significant fondness for the agency. And so I had applied and they picked me up relatively quickly. And I thought, you know, there is a world in which I would want to stay on the law enforcement side, not the intel side. Let me figure this out. I may absolutely fall in love with it. And I did love it. And I would say in my early professional career, the experience of having been working the cases that I worked, I worked on the high intensity drug trafficking area task force. So I worked in Washington, D.C. with Metropolitan Police. A ton, I worked in Maryland, predominantly with Baltimore P.D. or Maryland State Police. and doing a lot of joint cases, the inspection services often invited to do a lot of joint cases. And so, you know, partnering with 20-year police officers who've been working narcotics cases in D.C. or in Baltimore, like the things that I learned, the things that I saw, the arrests and search warrants that I was a part of are kind of most powerful memories that I have. It's where I have a tremendous love and appreciation for those who choose to serve their communities. It's where I think I grew a lot of my empathy and sadness for those who are fighting addiction and are the victims of large scale drunk traffickers who prey on people who are facing challenges, whatever they may be. And it was a place where I was able to see kind of some of at times people's very darkest moments. And I think that's been very informative for me throughout my career. And so I was grateful for the opportunity to serve in that way for a couple of years. And before ultimately, CIE called and said, hey, remember",
