    "turn_text": "If you're screaming at your ass that you've done something terribly wrong. But it's about developing trust and developing an authentic trust and which can be tricky because you're not always telling them the whole truth, right? But finding threads of commonality, finding threads of what's important to that person. And I think there's different methodologies about kind of what leads someone to be willing to commit espionage. And what I always found is that the people that I worked with who were providing me with information that, you know, I was reporting back to Washington that was helping inform us on a whole host of different issues, via counterterrorism or drug trafficking or, you know, foreign countries, nuclear programs. The motivation was generally one of kind of common purpose. And, you know, broadly speaking, our two countries do not get along. Our two countries don't have good diplomatic relationships. And this person thinks that, that and believes that they can do right by the world or by the country that they love, their own country, by making sure that there isn't some catastrophic misunderstanding. And",
