    "turn_text": "person you elected and why it matters. And I had, you know, I think a smaller portion of that with the first impeachment. I had been part of a group where after the allegations came out that then President Trump had tried to withhold vital military aid to Ukraine and were turned for political favors, there was a group of us that from a national security perspective framed out why this was potentially an impeachable offense and required an impeachment inquiry. Broadly credited or blamed depending on your perspective for unleashing the first impeachment. And the day that that came out and certainly the day that we voted on impeachment, I thought that I had essentially lost my reelection. And my commitment was if I believe that I'm doing the right thing and without a shadow of doubt, then I have to explain it to people because I owe it to my constituents to explain to them. And if I want them to elect me as a leader, that I need to show leadership and explain this. And so one of the best meetings that I've done during my time in Congress was the Tea Party of one of my counties, right after that, invited me to come to their meeting. And I don't know if they ever anticipated that I would come, but they were gracious enough or perhaps looking for an argument enough to invite me. And I went, it was an incredibly important conversation. And I I didn't necessarily convince anyone that I was right, but I did have, you know, even at that meeting, the gentleman who had invited me kept saying, like, it is wonderful that she's here. We don't have to agree with her, but she believes in this. Let's hear why. And I think that, frankly, far too infrequently members of Congress, they say, well, this is where my base is, and they go to the base. And my argument, literal argument back to people when I've had this conversation is like they've elected you to dig into these hard work topics. Like they've elected you to find answers to problems. They've selected you to show leadership.",
