  {
    "episode_id": 92596,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92596.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 185,
    "turn_text": "of course is about defending democracy and supporting our counterparts in the world. But I think beyond that, it's also about what's in the best interests of the United States and certainly how do we ensure that we're not drawn into a much larger conflict at some point down the line. And so making that argument, making sure people understand the NATO alliance, what our obligations would be, you know, the Russians ever sort of crossed into a NATO country. I think that's an important piece. But one of the things that I would mention, perhaps even relates to, and this goes back to kind of a prior topic, the issue of fentanyl trafficking, the issue of drug trafficking, that is, you'll hear a lot of my colleagues talk about the fact that fentanyl is killing people across the country, and of course it is. You'll hear people kind of want to use it to blame this person or blame that person. But they're not actually coming up with any solutions or even recognition of the fact that this is a problem. And so this is a place where, and certainly I want people to know about it, but I don't think it's as well known as it, otherwise could be or should be, that with the NDAA, I had a piece of legislation focused on combating fentanyl. And it would require DHS to kind of develop and find new technology that would help them target and detect illicit phenol from a drug trafficking standpoint. Phenol is so potent and can be trafficked relatively easily. And even when it relates to our interdiction efforts requiring DHS to work in concert with other agencies, be it within the intelligence world or other law enforcement agencies. This is a important parcel of how you get at this problem. And so there's a lot of people who are saying fentanyl, fentanyl, fentanyl, which is like a deadly problem. But let's think about all of the other downstream impacts. You've got transnational criminal organizations making a ton of money off of fentanyl trafficking that has destabilizing factors in other countries. issues of surges of immigration and migration. We, of course, have a domestic issue here where we're seeing record overdose levels in Virginia and elsewhere, where not only are people overdosing more, but those overdoses can be so much more fatal because of the potency and the lethality of phenol. And so this is an example, I think, where my national security framework like I view this issue as one that impacts us domestically for obvious reasons but also has major national security implications even in terms of regional stability and rather than kind of pointing fingers and you know just talking you know border security in a really reductive conversation like well how are we actually going to get at this relatively unique threat among all the different types of potential drugs being trafficked or attempting to be trafficked into the country.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
