    "turn_text": "works going well. Oh, yeah. And I got a work trip, right? And your work trip, someplace exotic and amazing. Oh, yeah, it's good. All a little jet lag. You know, and you don't seek attention. You know that what you're doing is, you know, some days incredibly exciting and incredibly rewarding. And, you know, some days it's preparing for the exciting and the rewarding. And so to be in a position where now you actually have to talk about yourself, you actually have to kind of try to get people to vote for you. It's a total, total switch. And for me, I guess the advice that I would give is what motivated you to work in service to our country. And, you know, for me, it was I want to answer questions. I want to keep people safe. I want to do something of value, I believe, in our country. in such a profound and deep way. And I think this is the place where I can help ensure that the lives of the people around me are unencumbered by threats they may never even know exist. And I like the idea that if I take on some of those stresses or if I work to try and figure out a problem, they don't even have to ever know it was a problem in a very sort of simplistic way. And I think that some of that drives me within politics is People are a bit more aware of the problems of their day-to-day, the challenges that they face, but how can I do something to try and make things better for people and serve people? And in politics, it is a two-party system. You do have to kind of pick a lane and define yourself a bit more. But I think that Alyssa Socken, one of my dearest friends in Congress and the former CIA officer, you know, the two of us are very clear that, you know, we didn't, you never knew people's affiliation, right? Or at",
